				<table cellpadding=5 cellspacing=0 border=0>
					<tr>
						<td style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold" colspan="2">
							Blood Contamination Probed in Ga., Fla.
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td style="font-size: 11pt;"> 
							
							<a href="#" OnClick="openpic('photos/BLOOD_CONTAMINATION0.jpg')"><IMG SRC="/DSDSMULTIMEDIA/photos/BLOOD_CONTAMINATION0.jpg" width="250" border="0" align="left"></a>
							A blood collection technician handles a blood donation from Jerry Bennett, of Snellville, Ga., at the American Red Cross in Atlanta, Friday, Jan. 31, 2003. Hospitals in Georgia and Northern Florida were warned Friday to halt use of some blood from the American Red Cross because it was contaminated with unidentified white particles. (AP Photo/John Bazemore)
							 
							<br>
							<br>
							06:41 AM EST February 01, 2003 
							<br>
							The Associated Press<br><br><br>ATLANTA<p class="nitfp">Officials from the American Red Cross and Food and Drug Administration are investigating a mysterious contaminant in bags of blood in Georgia and northern Florida, even as the manufacturer of the bags claimed the material was blood related.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Hospitals in Georgia and northern Florida were warned Friday to halt use of some blood from the Red Cross because it contained unidentified white particles.</p>
<p class="nitfp">The problem was "likely with the bags" and not with the blood itself, said Chris Hrouda, the Red Cross' chief executive officer for blood services in the southern region. He said only blood in bags manufactured by Baxter International Inc. had been found to be contaminated.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Dr. Leslie Holness of the FDA said the type of bags affected were an older product being phased out by the Red Cross, and added that only the southern region is still using the bags.</p>
<p class="nitfp">But a spokeswoman for Baxter said the company's investigation showed the substance was "biologic in nature and most likely blood related."</p>
<p class="nitfp">"You're talking about living cellular material - it needs to be handled under pretty precise circumstances," Deborah Spak said.</p>
<p class="nitfp">The Red Cross reported that the particles are not infectious agents and that no harmful effects in patients have been reported. It has identified the location of all the possibly contaminated blood and was working with a maker of blood bags and the FDA to find out how the contamination happened.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Officials said it is probably not related to sabotage.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"We haven't ruled anything out, but it's hard to fathom that it could be that," Hrouda said.</p>
<p class="nitfp">The contaminant, which is not organic, can be seen with the naked eye and is practically translucent, said Dr. Christopher D. Hillyer, an Emory University professor who works with the Red Cross. It is not uniform, with some bags containing more than others.</p>
<p class="nitfp">The Red Cross said so far 110 of the approximately 4,000 units at its regional center were contaminated. The agency provides blood to 140 hospitals in Georgia and northern Florida, but it didn't know how much, if any, of this blood was affected.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Hillyer said the blood may turn out to be usable after it is tested for potential problems.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Mary Malarkey, director of case management for the FDA's Center for Biologics Evaluation and Research, said the Red Cross plans to ask the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention to test the contaminant.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"Blood is available for emergency surgeries," said Marcy Blount, spokeswoman for the Red Cross. "We are asking hospitals not to use some of the blood in their inventories while we are having a quality assurance review."</p>
<p class="nitfp">Members of the Georgia Hospital Association were forced to postpone elective surgeries and use backup blood for emergencies. There are 185 hospitals in this association, but not all get blood from the Red Cross.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"We're taking more of a cautious approach and putting the blood on hold," said Kevin Bloye, a spokesman with the association. "For some of our hospitals without a large supply of blood in reserve, that's a big problem."</p>

